@@ -2,8 +2,38 @@
 
 By default, proprio data will be recorded in the hdf5 file. To turn on image data recording, `robolab.constants.RECORD_IMAGE_DATA` needs to be set to True.
 
+Recorded tensors are transferred from the simulator GPU to CPU at the recorder
+boundary. Policy input tensors remain on the simulator GPU; only the persistent
+recording copy moves. The image recorder keeps `image_obs` and `proprio_obs` by
+default, excluding the viewport-only render group.
+
+Choose an HDF5 storage profile according to the workload:
+
+| Profile | Image codec | Image chunk | Flush | Use case |
+| --- | --- | --- | ---: | --- |
+| `fast` (default with images) | none | one frame | 8 steps | High-concurrency collection to local NVMe |
+| `balanced` | LZF | one frame | 16 steps | Smaller files with moderate CPU cost |
+| `compact` | gzip | automatic | 32 steps | Archival when write latency is unimportant |
+
+Use `--record-storage-profile PROFILE` and optionally
+`--record-flush-interval N`. A smaller flush interval reduces host-memory peaks
+and repeated concatenation, but writes more often. Setting the interval to `0`
+disables periodic flushing and is unsafe for long image-bearing episodes.
+
+The fast profile intentionally trades disk space for collection throughput.
+Write to node-local NVMe, then compress or convert the completed dataset
+offline. Shared filesystems and gzip can become the bottleneck even after GPU
+memory is fixed.
+
 Recorded episodes can be played back — see [Replaying Recorded Episodes](replay.md).
 
+Validate a completed multi-env file before moving it into a training dataset:
+
+```bash
+python scripts/validate_recording.py run_0.hdf5 \
+  --expected-demos 10 --expected-steps 1800
+```
+
 ## Single-Env vs Multi-Env Recording
 
 RoboLab supports running multiple parallel environments per task. The recording system handles both cases:
@@ -20,7 +50,7 @@ RoboLab supports running multiple parallel environments per task. The recording
 - Subtask logs: `log_{run_idx}_env{env_id}.json` (one per env)
 - Each env has its own subtask state machine, event tracker, and termination state
 - Envs terminate independently — when an env succeeds, it freezes while others continue
-- HDF5 recording uses auto-flush (every 500 steps) with concurrent per-env streaming — each env's buffer is flushed to its own demo without interfering with other envs
+- HDF5 recording uses concurrent per-env streaming. The image profile selects an 8, 16, or 32-step default; low-dimensional recording keeps the 500-step default. Each env is assigned a stable demo index before its first flush.
 - Total episodes = `num_runs * num_envs`
 
 > **Tip:** Prefer increasing `--num_envs` for more episodes. Only increase `--num-runs` (default 1) if you run out of GPU memory with the desired `num_envs`. For example, to run 24 episodes when only 12 fit in VRAM: `--num_envs 12 --num-runs 2`.
